phy: qcom: edp: fix Glymur (v8) PLL tables and the HBR2 pixel clock divider

Two defects found on a Lenovo Yoga Slim 7 14Q8Y11 (X2 Plus, eDP on mdss_dp3,
panel limited to HBR2 x2 lanes):

1. The v8 PLL settings for RBR, HBR and HBR2 did not lock a usable link.
   HBR2 now matches what the firmware leaves in the PHY (register dump taken
   before the first write) and qmp_v8_dp_serdes_tbl_hbr2 of the combo PHY;
   RBR and HBR follow the combo PHY tables and are untested. HBR3 is
   unchanged. VCO_TUNE{1,2}_MODE0 and the SSC step become per-rate.

2. DP_PHY_VCO_DIV = 0x2 at HBR2 gives half the pixel clock the clock
   framework is told (30 Hz instead of 60 Hz, link trained, panel black).
   0x0 gives 60 Hz and a picture; 0x1 gives 40 Hz. Only the HBR2 entry is
   changed; the other three are untested and kept.

Not for upstream as is: RBR/HBR/HBR3 need a platform that can reach them.
---
--- a/drivers/phy/qualcomm/phy-qcom-edp.c
+++ b/drivers/phy/qualcomm/phy-qcom-edp.c
@@ -286,8 +286,21 @@
 	0x00, 0x00, 0xa0, 0x00, 0x0a, 0x26, 0x0a, 0x03, 0x37, 0x03, 0x02, 0x02, 0x04,
 };
 
+/*
+ * DP_PHY_VCO_DIV codes for RBR, HBR, HBR2, HBR3. The encoding is not the v4
+ * one (1 = /2, 2 = /4, 0 = /6 of the link bit rate).
+ *
+ * Only HBR2 is measured (Lenovo Yoga Slim 7 14Q8Y11, Glymur eDP PHY, HBR2 x2,
+ * 1920x1200@60, frames counted over 10 s): 0x0 gives 60 Hz, i.e. the 1350 MHz
+ * the clock framework is told (5.4 G / 4); 0x1 gives 40 Hz (5.4 G / 6) and the
+ * previous 0x2 gave 30 Hz (5.4 G / 8) and a black panel.
+ *
+ * RBR, HBR and HBR3 are untested and left as they were: the HBR3 value agrees
+ * with the measurement if the divider follows the bit rate (0x1 = /6), but by
+ * the same reading 0x0 would be /4 where RBR and HBR need /2.
+ */
 static const u8 edp_phy_vco_div_cfg_v8[4] = {
-	0x00, 0x00, 0x02, 0x01,
+	0x00, 0x00, 0x00, 0x01,
 };
 
 static int qcom_edp_phy_init(struct phy *phy)
@@ -916,13 +929,17 @@
 
 	switch (dp_opts->link_rate) {
 	case 1620:
-	case 2700:
-	case 8100:
-		step1 = 0x5b;
+		step1 = 0x83;
 		step2 = 0x02;
 		break;
 
+	case 2700:
 	case 5400:
+		step1 = 0x18;
+		step2 = 0x02;
+		break;
+
+	case 8100:
 		step1 = 0x5b;
 		step2 = 0x02;
 		break;
@@ -952,32 +969,53 @@
 	u32 lock_cmp2_mode0;
 	u32 code1_mode0;
